```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: mswitch1
diagnosis_text:
The affected component is the VLAN 10 trunk subinterface eth0.10 on mswitch1. In
this topology the trunk link between switch1 (the access switch where PC4 and PC5
reside) and mswitch1 (the multilayer switch) is carried over the physical link
link_msw1_sw1. Two dot1q subinterfaces — eth0.10 and eth0.20 — are created on
that trunk and enslaved to br_vlan10 and br_vlan20 respectively, providing separate
broadcast domains for VLAN 10 (172.16.10.0/24) and VLAN 20 (172.16.20.0/24).

The fault is that mswitch1's eth0.10 subinterface has been brought
administratively down (its kernel flags show <BROADCAST,MULTICAST> with no UP
bit, confirmed by `ip link show eth0.10` returning state DOWN). As a result,
any VLAN 10 frame arriving from switch1 finds a dead port on the mswitch1 side;
br_vlan10 on mswitch1 has no path back toward switch1, so ARP requests from PC4
and PC5 never reach router0's 172.16.10.1 gateway and replies cannot return.
The parent interface eth0 and the eth0.20 subinterface are both fully UP, which
is why VLAN 20 traffic (laptop0 → 172.16.20.1) is completely unaffected.
Router0's eth0.10 (holding 172.16.10.1/24) and mswitch1's eth4.10 are also UP,
so the fault is isolated exclusively to mswitch1.eth0.10 being administratively
shut.

Root cause: mswitch1's VLAN 10 trunk subinterface (eth0.10) was administratively
shut down, severing the only L2 path between switch1 and br_vlan10 on mswitch1.

Corrective fix: On mswitch1, run `ip link set eth0.10 up`. To make it
persistent across restarts, add the equivalent `ip link set eth0.10 up` line to
mswitch1's startup script.
=== END ===
```